# 🎮 HAFFY Controller X1
### A Custom-Built USB Game Controller Powered by Raspberry Pi Pico

<p align="center">
  <strong>Built Not Bought. Designed to Play.</strong>
</p>

---

## 🚀 Overview

**HAFFY Controller X1** is a custom-built USB game controller developed using the Raspberry Pi Pico microcontroller.

The project was created to explore embedded systems, USB HID communication, analog input processing, hardware interfacing, and custom controller design.

Unlike commercially available controllers, X1 is designed and assembled from individual electronic components, including analog joystick modules, tactile switches, a multiplexer, and vibration motors.

The controller is housed in a custom 3D-printed enclosure and has been successfully tested with PC games.

## ✨ Features

- 🎮 Custom USB game controller
- 🕹️ Dual analog joystick support
- 🔘 16-button matrix
- 🎯 L3 and R3 joystick button support
- 🔌 USB HID gamepad communication
- 🔄 74HC4051 analog multiplexer
- 📳 Dual vibration motor hardware
- 🎨 Custom 3D-printed controller enclosure
- 🖥️ Compatible with Windows gamepad input
- 🧪 Tested with Ghost Recon Breakpoint

## 🛠️ Hardware Components

| Component | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Raspberry Pi Pico | Main microcontroller |
| 74HC4051 | Analog signal multiplexing |
| Analog Joystick Modules | Movement and camera control |
| Tactile Push Buttons | Controller inputs |
| AO3400A MOSFETs | Vibration motor drivers |
| Vibration Motors | Haptic feedback |
| Custom PCB / Perfboard | Circuit assembly |
| USB Cable | Power and communication |
| 3D-Printed Enclosure | Controller body |

## 🧠 System Architecture

The Raspberry Pi Pico acts as the central controller.

```text
       ANALOG JOYSTICKS
               |
               v
        74HC4051 MUX
               |
               v
        RASPBERRY PI PICO
               |
       USB HID GAMEPAD
               |
               v
             PC GAME


       16-BUTTON MATRIX
               |
               v
        RASPBERRY PI PICO


       VIBRATION MOTORS
               |
               v
          MOSFET DRIVERS
               |
               v
        RASPBERRY PI PICO
```

## 🔌 Pin Configuration

### 74HC4051 Multiplexer

| Multiplexer Pin | Raspberry Pi Pico GPIO |
|---|---|
| S0 | GPIO 12 |
| S1 | GPIO 13 |
| S2 | GPIO 14 |
| SIG / Z | GPIO 26 (ADC0) |
| VCC | 3.3V |
| GND | GND |

### Analog Joystick Channels

| Multiplexer Channel | Function |
|---|---|
| Y0 | Left Joystick VRX |
| Y1 | Left Joystick VRY |
| Y2 | Right Joystick VRX |
| Y3 | Right Joystick VRY |

### Other Connections

- Button matrix: 16 tactile switches.
- L3: GPIO 2.
- R3: GPIO 3.
- Vibration motor drivers: GPIO 20 and GPIO 21.

*Note: Refer to the firmware source for the complete button-matrix pin assignments.*

## ⚙️ How It Works

### 1. Analog Joystick Processing

The controller uses two analog joysticks to provide movement and camera control.

Since multiple analog signals need to be read using the Pico, a 74HC4051 multiplexer is used.

The Pico selects the required channel using three digital selection pins and reads the selected analog signal through ADC0.

### 2. Button Input Processing

A 4×4 button matrix is used to read 16 tactile switches.

Additional joystick-click inputs are provided through L3 and R3.

### 3. USB HID Communication

The Raspberry Pi Pico processes the joystick and button inputs and communicates with the computer as a USB gamepad.

This allows compatible PC games to recognize the controller as a game input device.

### 4. Vibration System

Two vibration motors are controlled through AO3400A MOSFET driver circuits.

The current firmware includes button-triggered vibration functionality.

**Note:** This is not yet full game-generated force feedback or USB rumble support.

